---
// User guide. Rule for every session: any change to how a tool works updates this page (its section and
// "What's new") in the same commit; scripts/guide-check.mjs enforces it in the site test.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/containers.json';
import pallets from '../data/pallets.json';
import trailers from '../data/trailers.json';
import { boxTrucks } from '../data/box-trucks.js';
import { cargoVans } from '../data/cargo-vans.js';
import stamp from '../../ops/guide-stamp.json';
import { SITE_NAME } from '../site.js';
const updated = new Date(stamp.updated + 'T00:00:00Z').toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
const schema = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'HowTo', name: `How to use ${SITE_NAME}`, step: [
  { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Choose the container', text: 'Pick a container type or enter your own inside size, payload and door opening.' },
  { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Enter your boxes', text: 'For each box type enter length, width, height, weight and quantity, and how it may be turned.' },
  { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Read the plan', text: 'See how many boxes fit, the space and weight used, and step through the 3D loading plan.' },
  { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Adjust and share', text: 'Set loading steps or edit the plan by hand, then copy the link or print the plan.' },
] };
---
<Base title="How to Use the Load Planner" description="Step-by-step guide to Load Plan Calc: container and box inputs, carton orientation, automatic and semi-automatic loading, editing the 3D plan by hand, sharing and printing." schema={schema}>
  <section class="prose">
    <h1>How to use {SITE_NAME}</h1>
    <p class="lede">Everything the load planner can do, in the order you will use it. Last updated {updated}.</p>
    <nav class="chips" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#quick-start">Quick start</a>
      <a href="#container-loading-calculator">Container loading calculator</a>
      <a href="#pallets-first">Cartons on pallets first</a>
      <a href="#pallet-loading-calculator">Pallet loading calculator</a>
      <a href="#how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-container">Pallets in a container</a>
      <a href="#how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-53-foot-trailer">Pallets in a 53 ft trailer</a>
      <a href="#cbm-calculator">CBM calculator</a>
      <a href="#cubic-feet-calculator">Cubic feet calculator</a>
      <a href="#freight-density-calculator">Freight density calculator</a>
      <a href="#dimensional-weight-calculator">Dimensional weight calculator</a>
      <a href="#orientation">Carton orientation</a>
      <a href="#loading-method">Automatic or step by step</a>
      <a href="#stacking">Interlocked or column stacks</a>
      <a href="#edit-by-hand">Editing by hand</a>
      <a href="#results">Reading the result</a>
      <a href="#several-containers">Several containers</a>
      <a href="#share">Share, save and print</a>
      <a href="#whats-new">What's new</a>
    </nav>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="quick-start">
    <h2>Quick start</h2>
    <ol class="plain">
      <li>Open the <a href="/container-loading-calculator/">container loading calculator</a> (or the <a href="/pallet-loading-calculator/">pallet loading calculator</a> for cartons on one pallet, or <a href="/how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-container/">how many pallets fit in a container</a> for whole pallets, or <a href="/how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-53-foot-trailer/">how many pallets fit in a 53 ft trailer</a> for a truck). It already shows a worked example, so you can see a result before typing anything.</li>
      <li>Choose your container under <strong>Container type</strong> (<strong>Container or trailer</strong> on the container loading calculator, <strong>Trailer model</strong> on the 53 ft trailer page), or pick <strong>Custom size</strong> and type its inside size.</li>
      <li>Under <strong>Boxes</strong>, change the example cartons to yours: size, weight and quantity. Add more box types with <strong>+ Add box type</strong>.</li>
      <li>The result updates as you type: how many boxes fit, the volume and weight used, and a 3D plan you can turn and step through. On a phone, while you edit the boxes and the result is below the screen, a bar at the bottom shows how many boxes are loaded; tap it to jump to the result.</li>
      <li>Use <strong>Copy link to this plan</strong> to send it to a colleague, or <strong>Print plan</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="container-loading-calculator">
    <h2>Container loading calculator</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/container-loading-calculator/">container loading calculator</a> fits mixed carton sizes into a shipping container and shows the plan in 3D. Everything is calculated in your browser; your inputs are not sent to us.</p>

    <h3>Units</h3>
    <p>Switch between <strong>cm · kg</strong> and <strong>in · lb</strong> at the top of the form. Values are stored in millimetres and kilograms, so switching back and forth never changes your numbers. Your choice is remembered on this device.</p>

    <h3>Container</h3>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Container or trailer:</strong> {data.containers.map((c) => c.name).join(', ')}. These presets fill in the inside length, width and height, the door opening and the maximum payload from the carrier's published figures (see <a href="/shipping-container-dimensions/">shipping container dimensions</a>). The list also has three 53' dry vans: {trailers.trailers.map((t) => t.model).join(', ')}, with the inside length, width and height Hyundai Translead publishes. Their door and payload fields stay empty, because the maker gives no figures for them; enter your own to have them checked. At the end of the list are eight rental box trucks: {boxTrucks.map((t) => t.name).join(', ')}. They fill in the inside length, width and height and the max load (as the payload) that each fleet prints on its truck page; the U-Haul trucks also fill in the door opening U-Haul prints, while Penske prints none, so its door fields stay empty. Penske's figures are “up to” sizes and both fleets call their measurements approximate, so measure your truck for a tight load (see <a href="/box-truck-dimensions/">box truck dimensions</a>). Last come four Mercedes-Benz Sprinter cargo vans ({cargoVans.map((v) => v.short).join('; ')}): they fill in the cargo bed length, the width between the rear wheel arches (the arches stand inside the floor, so the planner uses that width over the whole length), the interior height, the rear door opening and the payload of the 2500 Standard Output version, as Mercedes-Benz prints them (see <a href="/cargo-van-dimensions/">cargo van dimensions</a>).</li>
      <li><strong>Inside size, payload, door:</strong> folded under the list to keep the form short on a phone; its line shows the figures in use (for example “1,203.2 × 235 × 270 cm · 28,600 kg · door 234 × 259.7”). Tap it to see or change them. It opens by itself when you choose <strong>Custom size</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Custom size:</strong> chosen automatically as soon as you change any container figure. Use it for your exact unit; the CSC plate or your booking has the real figures.</li>
      <li><strong>Max payload:</strong> the most cargo weight allowed. Leave it empty for no weight limit.</li>
      <li><strong>Door width and height:</strong> every box must pass the door the way it is stowed. Leave them empty to skip this check.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Boxes</h3>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Name, quantity, weight, length, width, height</strong> for each box type. Up to 10 box types, up to 20,000 boxes per type. Each type gets its own colour in the 3D view.</li>
      <li><strong>Orientation, limits, stop</strong> (folded at the bottom of each box card, with a one-line summary): which way the box may be turned (see <a href="#orientation">Carton orientation</a>), its stacking limits and, in a container, its delivery stop. It opens by itself when you choose the positions by hand.</li>
      <li><strong>Can carry boxes on top:</strong> untick for fragile cartons or crates; nothing will be stacked on them.</li>
      <li id="delivery-stops"><strong>Delivery stop</strong> (container calculators): for a load with several drops, give each box type the number of its stop in delivery order (1 = unloaded first). The planner then loads the highest stop first at the back wall and stop 1 last at the door, each stop in its own section that starts in front of the one before, so no stop is blocked by a later one. Boxes without a stop are treated as one more stop after the numbered ones and go to the very back. Leave every stop empty (or give all the same number) for an ordinary plan. Stops work in fully automatic mode, also when the load needs several containers (each container is loaded stop by stop); in semi-automatic mode your own steps decide the order. The loading list, the step table and the loading step slider name the stops.</li>
      <li id="stacking-limits"><strong>Max boxes on top</strong> and <strong>Max weight on top</strong> (shown while “Can carry boxes on top” is ticked): the stacking limit printed on many cartons, as a number of boxes or a weight. Leave them empty for no limit. Max boxes on top counts the boxes in the tallest column standing on the box (2 means at most 2 boxes above it, so 3 high counting itself). Max weight on top is everything resting on the box, directly or higher up; a box standing on two boxes passes its weight to them in proportion to how much of its base rests on each. Automatic plans, Fill the rest and hand edits all keep both limits, and the independent check tests them on every plan. A note under the result names the limits in use.</li>
      <li><strong>✕</strong> removes a box type. <strong>+ Add box type</strong> copies the size of the last type to save typing.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 id="import">Import a box list from Excel</h3>
    <p>Instead of typing each box, you can bring in your packing list:</p>
    <ol class="plain">
      <li>Download the template (<a href="/load-plan-template.xlsx" download>Excel</a> or <a href="/load-plan-template.csv" download>CSV</a>) under the box list, or use your own sheet. One row per box type, a header row with <strong>Name, Length, Width, Height, Weight, Quantity</strong>, and if you like <strong>This side up</strong> (yes/no), <strong>Stackable</strong> (yes/no; empty means yes), <strong>Stop</strong> (delivery stop number), <strong>Max boxes on top</strong> and <strong>Max weight on top</strong> (empty means no limit; the weight in the header's unit, for example Max weight on top (kg)). Common other headings work too, such as SKU, Qty, Pcs, L, W, H or Gross weight.</li>
      <li>Put the unit in the header in brackets: Length (cm), Length (mm), Length (in), Weight (kg), Weight (lb). A column without a unit is read in the units selected at the top of the form (cm and kg, or in and lb), and the note after importing says so.</li>
      <li>Tap <strong>Import from Excel / CSV</strong> and choose the file (.xlsx or .csv; the first sheet is read). Or select the rows in Excel or Google Sheets, copy them, tap <strong>Paste from Excel</strong>, paste, and tap <strong>Use these rows</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The imported rows replace the box list, and the plan updates at once. A note lists what was imported, which units were assumed and which rows were skipped (for example a row without a height). Up to 10 box types and 20,000 boxes per type are read. Old .xls files are not read: save them as .xlsx or CSV first. The file is read in your browser and is not sent to us.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="pallets-first">
    <h2>Cartons on pallets first</h2>
    <p>In the <a href="/container-loading-calculator/">container loading calculator</a>, <strong>Put the cartons on pallets first</strong> (folded under the box list) plans the usual two steps in one go: cartons onto pallets, then the loaded pallets into the container.</p>
    <ol class="plain">
      <li>Enter your cartons in the box list as usual (stacking pattern and stacking limits apply to the pallets too).</li>
      <li>Open <strong>Put the cartons on pallets first</strong> and choose the <strong>Pallet</strong> (EPAL and CHEP sizes with their makers' height, own weight and safe working load, or a custom size), the <strong>Max height incl. pallet</strong> (your own limit; 1,800 mm is only an example), the <strong>Pallet weight</strong> and the <strong>Max load per pallet</strong>. Choose the <strong>Layer pattern</strong>: <strong>Most cartons per layer</strong> (the layer that holds the most, which may mix two ways of turning the carton, for example a pinwheel) or <strong>All cartons the same way</strong> (a plain grid, simpler to build, sometimes fewer cartons), and the <strong>Layers</strong>: <strong>Same every layer</strong> (straight columns, the default) or <strong>Alternating</strong> (interlocked layers, used where they tie together). Tick <strong>Loaded pallets may stand on each other</strong> to allow two high.</li>
      <li>Tap <strong>Put on pallets and load the pallets</strong>. Each box type is built into its own pallets in even layers, the way a warehouse builds them: one layer pattern centred on the pallet, repeated (or alternated) up to the height, load and stacking limits; the last pallet holds full layers and a part layer on top. Changing the layer options rebuilds the pallets at once.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The box list then holds the loaded pallets (size of the pallet, height of the load including the pallet, gross weight including the pallet's own weight, and how many), and the whole plan, 3D view, several containers, size comparison, loading list and printing work on the pallets. A table above the list shows, for each carton type, the cartons per pallet (with the Ti × Hi when the layers are even), the number of pallets, and the height and gross weight of a full and of the last pallet. A carton type that does not fit on the pallet stays loose in the list. <strong>Back to the cartons</strong> puts your carton list back. Copy link and Saved plans keep both.</p>
    <p>In the 3D view of the container each loaded pallet shows its wooden deck and every carton on it, turned with the pallet. <strong>View</strong> in a row of the table opens that pallet on its own in 3D (full pallet and last pallet), with its cartons, Ti × Hi, height and weight. There you can set <strong>Cartons per pallet</strong> to fewer than the most that fit (for a weak carton, a lower pallet or a customer's rule) and tap <strong>Rebuild the pallets</strong>; leave it empty for the most. <strong>Edit this pallet by hand in the pallet calculator</strong> opens the pallet loading calculator in a new tab with the same carton, pallet and count, where every carton can be moved or turned by hand; changes made there stay on that page.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="pallet-loading-calculator">
    <h2>Pallet loading calculator</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/pallet-loading-calculator/">pallet loading calculator</a> stacks cartons on one pallet and shows how many fit, the Ti-Hi and a 3D plan. It uses the same planner as the container calculator, so boxes, orientation, loading steps, hand editing, sharing and printing work as described in the other sections of this guide; only the pallet inputs and three results are different.</p>
    <h3>Pallet</h3>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Pallet size:</strong> {pallets.pallets.map((x) => x.name).join(', ')}. A preset fills in the pallet length, width and height from the maker's published figures (see <a href="/pallet-sizes/">standard pallet sizes</a>) and sets <strong>Max load weight</strong> to the pallet's safe working load and <strong>Pallet weight</strong> to the weight the maker prints (EPAL's approximate figure; CHEP prints none for its 48 × 40 in pallet, so it stays empty). The 48 × 40 in pallet is entered as exactly 48 × 40 in.</li>
      <li><strong>Custom size:</strong> chosen automatically as soon as you change the pallet length, width or height.</li>
      <li><strong>Max height incl. pallet:</strong> how tall the loaded pallet may be, measured from the floor. The value filled in at the start is only an example; enter the limit your racking, trailer, container door or customer sets. The pallet height is taken off, so the boxes get the rest.</li>
      <li><strong>Max load weight:</strong> the most the boxes on the pallet may weigh together. Change it to your own limit, or leave it empty for no limit.</li>
      <li><strong>Overhang per side:</strong> how far boxes may stick out past each edge of the pallet. 0 keeps every box on the deck.</li>
      <li><strong>Pallet height:</strong> the height of the empty pallet.</li>
      <li><strong>Pallet weight:</strong> the weight of the empty pallet, in kg or lb. It changes no count: it is only added to the boxes for <strong>Gross weight incl. pallet</strong>. Change it to your own pallet's weight, or leave it empty to count 0. Editing it keeps the preset, and it is saved in the share link.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Result</h3>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Boxes loaded</strong>, <strong>Volume used</strong> (of the space above the pallet up to the height limit), <strong>Weight loaded</strong>, <strong>Boxes left over</strong>, <strong>Space left</strong> and <strong>Weight left</strong> work as in the container calculator.</li>
      <li><strong>Ti × Hi (per layer × layers):</strong> with one box type, the boxes in one layer of a full pallet and the number of layers, for example “8 × 6” (48 per pallet). Interlocked layers that alternate between two counts show both, for example “8 / 7 × 6” (45 per pallet). If the weight limit stops the top layer short, it shows as, for example, “16 × 5 + 13”. When the boxes stand in different positions or the layers differ, no single Ti-Hi is shown and the note says why; choose “this side up” under Orientation for even layers. Not shown with more than one box type, in semi-automatic mode or while you edit by hand.</li>
      <li><strong>Gross weight incl. pallet:</strong> the boxes loaded plus the pallet weight, what the loaded pallet weighs, for example 540 kg + 25 kg = 565 kg for the default Euro pallet. Use it as the weight per pallet in <a href="/how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-container/">how many pallets fit in a container</a>. <strong>Weight loaded</strong> and <strong>Weight left</strong> stay the boxes only. The formula lines and the note above the loading list repeat the sum.</li>
      <li><strong>Pallets for your quantity:</strong> your quantity divided by the boxes on a full pallet, rounded up, with how many go on the last pallet (for example “2 full of 45 + 1 with 10”).</li>
      <li><strong>Pallet cube:</strong> the outside volume of the loaded pallet: the pallet footprint (or the cartons, where they overhang) × the height from the floor to the top of the load, pallet included, in m³ or ft³ with the other unit and the three sizes below it. For the default Euro pallet with 6 layers of 250 mm: 1.2 × 0.8 × (0.144 + 1.5) m = 1.578 m³ (55.74 ft³). Below the calculator, <a href="/pallet-loading-calculator/#how-many-boxes">How many boxes fit on a pallet?</a> explains the steps and lists cartons per pallet and the pallet cube for common carton sizes.</li>
      <li><strong>Stacking:</strong> interlocked or column stacks (see <a href="#stacking">Interlocked or column stacks</a>).</li>
      <li>With one box type, the plan is built layer by layer from the bottom, so a quantity smaller than a full pallet shows as full layers and a part layer on top. The loading step slider adds the boxes in that order.</li>
      <li><strong>3D view:</strong> the brown slab is the pallet; the outline is the load space (pallet plus overhang, up to the height limit). <strong>Front</strong> looks at the pallet's front edge.</li>
      <li><strong>Notes:</strong> boxes that do not fit on this pallet (they go on the next pallets: see Pallets for your quantity), the max load weight being reached, and the centre of gravity of the load: how far it is off the middle of the pallet along the length and across the width, and how high above the deck.</li>
      <li>When editing by hand, the push buttons are <strong>← Back edge</strong> and <strong>Front edge →</strong> instead of back wall and door; left and right are as seen from the front edge.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-container">
    <h2>How many pallets fit in a container</h2>
    <p><a href="/how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-container/">How many pallets fit in a container</a> is the container loading calculator set up for whole pallets: each loaded pallet is one box. Container type, units, results, loading steps, hand editing, sharing and printing work as described in the rest of this guide.</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Starting example:</strong> 50 loaded Euro pallets, each 1,100 mm high (pallet included) and 500 kg, one high in a 40' Standard: 25 fit. The height and weight are only an example; enter your own.</li>
      <li><strong>Pallet size (footprint):</strong> a list on each box card: {pallets.pallets.map((x) => x.name).join(', ')}. Picking one fills in the box length and width (the 48 × 40 in pallet as exactly 48 × 40 in); the height stays as you set it. Typing your own length or width switches it to <strong>Custom size</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Height:</strong> the loaded pallet from the floor to the top of the goods, pallet included. <strong>Weight:</strong> the gross weight of one loaded pallet.</li>
      <li><strong>One high or two high:</strong> leave <strong>Can carry boxes on top</strong> unticked to keep every pallet on the floor. Tick it to let pallets stack where two (or more) fit under the inside height; with the example, 50 then fit.</li>
      <li><strong>Orientation</strong> starts at “this side up”: pallets may be turned lengthwise or crosswise but are never tipped.</li>
      <li>Each pallet must pass the door as it is stowed; a loaded pallet taller than the door opening is left out and a note says so.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The page also has a table of how many of each pallet fit in each container, one and two high, computed by the same planner.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-53-foot-trailer">
    <h2>How many pallets fit in a 53 ft trailer</h2>
    <p><a href="/how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-53-foot-trailer/">How many pallets fit in a 53 ft trailer</a> is the same planner set up for whole pallets in a 53' dry van: each loaded pallet is one box. Units, boxes, results, loading steps, hand editing, sharing and printing work as described in the rest of this guide.</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Trailer model:</strong> {trailers.trailers.map((t) => `${t.model} (${t.Win} in wide)`).join(', ')}, all 52′ 6″ long and 110 in high inside, as Hyundai Translead prints them. Changing any size switches it to <strong>Custom size</strong> for your own trailer.</li>
      <li><strong>Max payload, door width and door height</strong> start empty: the maker publishes no figures for them. Empty means no weight limit and no door check. Enter your carrier's payload and your measured door opening to have them checked.</li>
      <li><strong>Starting example:</strong> 36 loaded 48 × 40 in pallets, each 50 in high (pallet included) and 1,500 lb, one high in the Composite: 30 fit, two across with the 48 in side across. The height and weight are only an example; enter your own.</li>
      <li><strong>Pallet size (footprint)</strong> on each box card, <strong>Height</strong>, <strong>Weight</strong>, <strong>Can carry boxes on top</strong> (two high) and <strong>Orientation</strong> work as on <a href="#how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-container">pallets in a container</a>. To force the 48 in side along the trailer, choose the positions allowed under Orientation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The page also has a table of how many of each pallet fit in each trailer model, one and two high, computed by the same planner.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="cbm-calculator">
    <h2>CBM calculator</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/cbm-calculator/">CBM calculator</a> adds up the volume of your cartons in cubic metres (CBM) and cubic feet, and shows what share of each container's inside volume that is. It is plain arithmetic; it does not plan where the cartons go.</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Unit (cm · mm · in):</strong> the unit for carton sizes. Sizes are kept in millimetres, so switching back and forth never changes your numbers. Your choice is remembered on this device.</li>
      <li><strong>Carton lines:</strong> for each carton size enter a name, the quantity and the outside length, width and height. Each line shows the volume of one carton and of the whole line. <strong>+ Add carton line</strong> adds another size (up to 10); <strong>Remove</strong> deletes a line. A line with an empty or zero size or quantity is left out, and a note says so.</li>
      <li><strong>Starting example:</strong> 400 cartons of 60 × 40 × 40 cm and 500 of 50 × 30 × 30 cm, 60.9 m³ in total.</li>
      <li><strong>Result:</strong> total volume in m³ (the headline), the same in cubic feet, the number of cartons, and for the 20', 40', 40' high cube and 45' high cube the share of the inside volume (inside length × width × height from Hapag-Lloyd's figures). Over 100% it shows how many containers the volume needs at least. On a phone, a bar at the bottom shows the total while the result is off screen.</li>
      <li><strong>The share is an upper bound.</strong> Rigid cartons leave gaps, so fewer fit than the percentage suggests. <strong>Plan in 3D</strong> on each container row opens the <a href="/container-loading-calculator/">container loading calculator</a> with that container and your cartons filled in (any side may face down, weight 0: add the weights there) for a real loading plan.</li>
      <li><strong>How the calculator works</strong> below the result shows the formula with your own numbers, updated as you type.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="cubic-feet-calculator">
    <h2>Cubic feet calculator</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/cubic-feet-calculator/">cubic feet calculator</a> works out the volume of one or more items in cubic feet, with the same total in cubic meters, cubic yards, litres and US gallons. It is plain arithmetic and updates as you type; for the share of each shipping container in cubic meters and a 3D plan, use the CBM calculator above.</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Unit (in · ft · cm · m · mm):</strong> the unit for all sizes. Sizes are kept in millimetres, so switching back and forth never changes your numbers. The page starts in inches; your choice is remembered on this device.</li>
      <li><strong>Item lines:</strong> for each size enter a name, the quantity and the length, width and height. Each line shows the cubic feet of one item and of the whole line. <strong>+ Add item line</strong> adds another size (up to 10); <strong>Remove</strong> deletes a line. A line with an empty or zero size or quantity is left out, and a note says so.</li>
      <li><strong>Starting example:</strong> 100 cartons of 24 × 18 × 18 in (4.5 ft³ each) and 50 boxes of 12 × 12 × 12 in (1 ft³ each), 500 ft³ in total.</li>
      <li><strong>Result:</strong> total cubic feet (the headline), cubic meters, cubic yards, litres and US gallons, the number of items, and the share of the inside volume of a 20' and 40' standard container, a 40' high cube and a 53' dry van (Hyundai Translead Composite). The share is volume only: boxes leave gaps, so fewer fit. On a phone, a bar at the bottom shows the total while the result is off screen.</li>
      <li><strong>Cubic feet to cubic meters converter:</strong> type a number in <strong>Cubic feet</strong> or in <strong>Cubic meters</strong>; the other box follows, and the line below shows the conversion and the same volume in cubic yards, cubic inches and litres. It starts at 100 ft³.</li>
      <li><strong>How the calculator works</strong> below shows the formula with your own numbers, updated as you type. The page also has tables of container and trailer volumes in cubic feet, common carton sizes and a conversion chart.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="freight-density-calculator">
    <h2>Freight density calculator</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/freight-density-calculator/">freight density calculator</a> divides a shipment's weight by the space it takes, for pallets, crates or cartons. It is plain arithmetic and updates as you type. It gives the density only, not a freight class.</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Units (in · lb or cm · kg):</strong> sizes in inches and weights in pounds, or centimetres and kilograms. Values are kept in millimetres and kilograms, so switching never changes the result. The page starts in in · lb in the US and cm · kg elsewhere; your choice is remembered on this device.</li>
      <li><strong>Lines:</strong> one line per size of handling unit: a name, the number of <strong>Pieces</strong>, the outside <strong>Length</strong>, <strong>Width</strong> and <strong>Height</strong> as shipped (pallet included) and the <strong>Weight per piece</strong>. Each line shows the cube of one piece and the line's own density. <strong>+ Add line</strong> adds another size (up to 10); <strong>Remove</strong> deletes one. A line with an empty size, weight or count is left out, and a note says so.</li>
      <li><strong>Starting example:</strong> two pallets of 48 × 40 × 48 in at 500 lb and one of 48 × 40 × 36 in at 300 lb: 146.67 ft³, 1,300 lb, 8.86 lb/ft³.</li>
      <li><strong>Result:</strong> <strong>Freight density</strong> of the whole shipment (total weight ÷ total cube) in lb/ft³ with kg/m³ below it (or the other way round in metric), the total weight, the total cube and the number of pieces. On a phone, a bar at the bottom shows the density while the result is off screen.</li>
      <li><strong>How the calculator works</strong> shows each line's cube and weight and the division with your numbers. Tables give the density of a 48 × 40 in pallet at several heights and weights, and of a Euro pallet in kg/m³.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="dunnage-bag-calculator">
    <h2>Dunnage bag calculator</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/dunnage-bag-calculator/">dunnage bag calculator</a> checks whether an air bag can hold the cargo pushing on it, with the formulas of the IMO/ILO/UNECE CTU Code (annex 7, appendix 4). Results update as you type.</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Units (kg · m or lb · in):</strong> mass in kilograms or pounds, sizes in metres or inches, bursting pressure in bar or psi. Values are kept in kg, m and bar, so switching never changes them.</li>
      <li><strong>Cargo mass pushing on the bag:</strong> only the cargo that would move into the bag, for example the cargo behind it when braking.</li>
      <li><strong>Transport and direction:</strong> road forward, backward or sideways; rail lengthwise or sideways; sea areas A, B and C lengthwise or sideways. Each fills in the Code's horizontal and vertical acceleration coefficients.</li>
      <li><strong>Friction:</strong> pick the contact from the Code's friction table (for example a wooden pallet on a plywood floor, 0.45) or type your own factor; the calculation uses 75% of it, as the Code says.</li>
      <li><strong>Cargo height, length and width:</strong> used to check tipping; the length counts for forward and backward, the width for sideways.</li>
      <li><strong>Bag width, height and the gap it fills, and bag type</strong> (single use, safety factor 0.75, or reusable, 0.5).</li>
      <li><strong>Bursting pressure (optional):</strong> from the bag's label or maker. Without it you get the minimum bursting pressure needed; with it you also get the force your bag can take and whether it is strong enough.</li>
      <li><strong>Result:</strong> the bursting pressure needed, the force from the cargo (and whether sliding or tipping gives it), the contact area and your bag's capacity. "How the calculator works" shows every step with your numbers, and a table repeats the check for all transport modes, because the Code says to use the one with the largest force.</li>
      <li><strong>Starting example:</strong> 10 t on wooden pallets, road braking, a 1.0 × 1.8 m single-use bag in a 0.2 m gap: 45.37 kN and at least 0.60 bar.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="dimensional-weight-calculator">
    <h2>Dimensional weight calculator</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/dimensional-weight-calculator/">dimensional weight calculator</a> works out the dimensional (DIM) weight and the billable weight of one package from its size and actual weight. It is plain arithmetic and updates as you type.</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Units (in · lb / cm · kg):</strong> the unit for sizes and weight. Values are kept in millimetres and kilograms, so switching back and forth never changes your numbers. Your choice is remembered on this device.</li>
      <li><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>USPS: Priority Mail, Ground Advantage (retail), Parcel Select Destination Entry</strong> (the starting choice) or <strong>USPS: Priority Mail (commercial)</strong> follow the USPS Domestic Mail Manual: each side rounded up to a whole inch (centimetres are converted first), length × width × height, and only above 1,728 in³ divided by 139 and rounded up to a whole pound. <strong>Your carrier's divisor</strong> lets you enter any divisor in in³ per lb or cm³ per kg (it starts at the USPS 139); then nothing is rounded.</li>
      <li><strong>Length, width, height, actual weight:</strong> the outside size of the packed package and what it weighs. <strong>Packages:</strong> how many identical packages, for the totals.</li>
      <li><strong>Shape (USPS rules only):</strong> <strong>Not rectangular</strong> (a tube or a ball) measures each side at its widest and multiplies the volume by 0.785, as the DMM says. For commercial Priority Mail the DMM text for this case rounds sides off (to the nearest inch) and divides by 166, and the calculator follows it.</li>
      <li><strong>Starting example:</strong> an 18 × 14 × 12 in box of 10 lb: 3,024 in³ ÷ 139 = 21.76, rounded up to 22 lb billable.</li>
      <li><strong>Result:</strong> the billable weight (the headline, the greater of actual and dimensional weight, with the kg or lb equivalent), the dimensional weight (or “Not applied” at 1,728 in³ or less), which of the two you pay for, the weight it is priced at in whole pounds, and totals for several packages. A note appears when the dimensional weight is over 70 lb (the customer pays the 70-pound price) or the actual weight is over 70 lb. On a phone, a bar at the bottom shows the billable weight while the result is off screen.</li>
      <li><strong>How the calculator works</strong> below the result shows each step with your own numbers. The page also quotes the USPS rule and has a table of common box sizes.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="orientation">
    <h2>Carton orientation</h2>
    <p>A carton can sit in six positions: one of its three sides faces up, and its length runs either along the container (toward the door) or across it. For each box type open <strong>Orientation, limits, stop</strong> (folded at the bottom of the box card; its line shows what is set, for example “any side down · no stacking limit”) and choose under <strong>Orientation</strong>:</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Automatic: any side can face down</strong> — the planner may use all six positions and picks the mix that loads the most.</li>
      <li><strong>Automatic: this side up</strong> — the height stays vertical; the carton is only turned flat.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose the positions allowed…</strong> — six small drawings appear, one per position (the arrow under each drawing points along the container toward the door). Tap a drawing to allow or block that position. Leave one ticked to fix the position completely; the last one cannot be unticked.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The <strong>Positions used</strong> column of the results table shows which positions the plan used and how many boxes are in each.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="loading-method">
    <h2>Automatic or step by step</h2>
    <p>Under <strong>More options</strong> (folded under the box list; its line shows the method and stacking pattern in use, and it opens by itself when a shared link uses other settings than the page's own), then <strong>Loading method</strong>:</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Fully automatic</strong> — the planner chooses the order, place and position of every box to load as much as possible. Tick <strong>Load in list order</strong> if the first box type must go in before the others.</li>
      <li><strong>Semi-automatic</strong> — you set the loading steps and the planner places the boxes of each step, from the back wall toward the door. When you switch, one step per box type is filled in for you.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each step has:</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Box type</strong> and <strong>How many</strong> (leave empty to load all that are left of that type).</li>
      <li><strong>Position</strong> — automatic (the best of the positions allowed for that box) or one fixed position for this step only.</li>
      <li><strong>Start a new section in front of earlier steps</strong> — the step starts in front of everything already loaded, so its boxes are not tucked into gaps behind. Use it to keep orders, stops or destinations together.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Move steps with ↑ ↓, remove them with ✕, add up to 20 with <strong>+ Add step</strong>. <strong>Then load whatever is left automatically</strong> finishes with the boxes no step took; untick it to load only what your steps ask for.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="stacking">
    <h2>Interlocked layers or column stacks</h2>
    <p>Under <strong>More options</strong>, then <strong>Stacking pattern</strong>, in the container and pallet calculators:</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Interlocked (stable)</strong> — the layers alternate between two patterns, so boxes in one layer bridge the joints of the layer below and tie the load together, as in cross-stacking by hand. On a pallet the whole load is one stack of interlocked layers. In a container the load is built in walls of equal length from the back wall, each wall's layers mirrored across the width, so it can be loaded wall by wall; the space in front of the last full wall is filled automatically. It works for one box type that can carry boxes on top, in fully automatic mode; otherwise the plan uses column stacks and the result says so. It is the default in the container and pallet loading calculators.</li>
      <li><strong>Each box rests on at least</strong> (interlocked only) — 100%, 90%, 80% or 70% of its base. At 100% every box stands fully on boxes below. Below 100% a box may bridge a small gap, but that share of its base and its centre must rest on boxes below. 80% is filled in at the start; choose 100% for soft or weak cartons. If no interlocked pattern fits at the share you chose, the plan uses column stacks and suggests a lower share.</li>
      <li><strong>Column stacks (most boxes)</strong> — every layer is the same, so boxes stand in columns: often a few more boxes, but the columns are not tied together and have to be held by wrapping or straps. The pallets-in-a-container and 53 ft trailer pages start with column stacks, because loaded pallets are not cross-stacked.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The <strong>Stacking</strong> figure in the result says which pattern the plan uses, how many boxes each interlocked layer holds, what share of the boxes above the first layer bridge two or more boxes below, and how many more boxes column stacks would load when that is more. The formula lines repeat the layer counts and, in a container, the wall length.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="edit-by-hand">
    <h2>Editing the plan by hand</h2>
    <p>Under the 3D view:</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Edit this plan by hand</strong> keeps the current plan and lets you change it.</li>
      <li><strong>Load by hand from empty</strong> starts with an empty container and every box under “Not loaded”.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Select a box</h3>
    <p>Tap or click a box in the 3D view; it is outlined in yellow. On a phone, small boxes are easier to reach with <strong>‹ ›</strong>, which step through the boxes in loading order. The panel shows the box's size and where it stands.</p>
    <h3>Change a box</h3>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Turn:</strong> tap one of the positions shown (only the positions that box may use). It turns where it stands if there is room.</li>
      <li><strong>Push:</strong> ← Back wall, Door →, Left, Right, ↑ Up, ↓ Down. The box slides until it meets a wall or another box. Left and right are as seen from the door.</li>
      <li><strong>Pick a new spot:</strong> green boxes show every free spot where it fits; tap one to move it there.</li>
      <li><strong>Remove:</strong> takes the box out, together with any boxes standing on it. They are listed under “Not loaded”.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Place boxes that are not loaded</h3>
    <ol class="plain">
      <li>Under <strong>Not loaded</strong>, tap <strong>Place by hand</strong> next to a box type.</li>
      <li>Choose a <strong>Position</strong>, or leave it on automatic to use the best fit at each spot.</li>
      <li>Tap a green spot in the 3D view, or <strong>Put it in the next free spot</strong> (nearest the back wall and the floor). Keep tapping to place more; <strong>Stop</strong> when done.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>Fill the rest automatically</strong> keeps every box you placed and loads the remaining boxes around them. <strong>Undo</strong> and <strong>Redo</strong> step through your changes. <strong>Back to automatic plan</strong> drops the hand edits.</p>
    <h3>What is checked</h3>
    <p>Every hand change is checked before it is made, with the same rules as an automatic plan: no overlapping boxes, every box stands fully on the floor or on boxes below (with interlocked layers chosen: at least the share of its base you set, with its centre over a box), nothing on a box that cannot carry others, only the positions a box may use, and every box passes the door the way it is stowed. A box with others standing on it cannot be turned or moved until they are moved. If a change breaks a rule, the planner tells you why instead of making it.</p>
    <p class="small">Changing the container or the boxes in the form starts again from an automatic plan; a note says that your hand edits were cleared.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="results">
    <h2>Reading the result</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Boxes loaded</strong> (for example “900 of 900”), <strong>Volume used</strong>, <strong>Weight loaded</strong> and <strong>Boxes left over</strong> (boxes you entered that are not in the plan).</li>
      <li><strong>Stacking</strong> — interlocked or column stacks, and how many boxes above the first layer bridge two or more boxes below (see <a href="#stacking">Interlocked or column stacks</a>).</li>
      <li><strong>Space left</strong> is the empty volume still in the container, and <strong>Payload left</strong> how much more weight it may take (“No limit set” when you left the payload empty).</li>
      <li><strong>Room left after this plan</strong>: for each box type, how many more of that type alone would still fit in the space left, keeping the plan exactly as it is, and whether space or payload is what stops it. Use it to see whether a few more cartons can still go in, or how much of another product you could add. It updates a moment after the plan.</li>
      <li><strong>Notes</strong> under the figures: boxes that do not fit, the weight limit being reached, steps that could not load everything they asked for, and the cargo's centre of gravity along the length. Keep heavy cargo low and the load spread evenly.</li>
      <li><strong>3D view:</strong> drag to turn, pinch or scroll to zoom. <strong>3D, Top, Side, Door</strong> jump to fixed views. <strong>⤢</strong> in the top corner shows the 3D view full screen with the view and step buttons; <strong>✕</strong> or Esc closes it. In a container or trailer, a tractor unit (cab, hood, wheels, fifth wheel) is drawn at the closed front end with the chassis, landing gear and rear axles under the load; for a box truck, its cab is drawn in front of the body. The door end has an orange frame marked <strong>DOOR</strong>, so you can always tell which end is which. The vehicle is a drawing for orientation, not to any truck's real size.</li>
      <li><strong>Loading step slider:</strong> drag it, or tap <strong>‹</strong> and <strong>›</strong> on each side to go back or forward one box at a time. The last box shown is outlined in white and named under the slider with its position. It shows the plan box by box in loading order, from the back wall toward the door, section by section and layer by layer from the floor up, in the order of the loading list. In semi-automatic mode it also tells you which step a box belongs to.</li>
      <li><strong>Tables:</strong> ordered (<strong>Still to load</strong> from the second container on), loaded and left over per box type, the loaded weight, and the positions used. In semi-automatic mode a second table shows each step's asked and loaded counts.</li>
      <li><strong>Loading list:</strong> the plan as loading instructions. In a container it is cut into sections from the back wall to the door (with interlocked layers, the walls of the plan; otherwise a section ends where no box crosses the line across the load, or runs about 1.2 m where boxes of different lengths overlap; in semi-automatic mode each step has its own sections). Each section is listed layer by layer from the floor up: box type, how many, position, and where they go (columns <strong>From back wall</strong> and <strong>From right wall</strong>: distances from the back wall and from the right-hand wall as seen from the door, in the units you chose), and the boxes <strong>Loaded so far</strong>. On a pallet the list runs layer by layer from the deck up, measured from the back and right-hand edges seen from the front. Each section shows the boxes and weight loaded so far; the loading step slider follows the same order. Long lists show the first sections and a <strong>Show all</strong> button. It is shown for automatic, semi-automatic and hand-edited plans. <strong>Show pictures</strong> adds a small 3D picture to each section: the load as it stands when that section is done, seen from the door side, with the section framed in orange (on a pallet, one picture per layer). Tap <strong>Hide pictures</strong> to remove them; the choice is remembered on this device.</li>
      <li><strong>Formula lines</strong> under the tables show the container volume, cargo volume, volume used, space left and weight with your numbers.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Every plan passes an independent check before it is shown. If a plan ever failed it, you would see a warning instead; please <a href="/contact/">tell us</a> the inputs. <a href="/how-we-calculate/">How we calculate</a> explains the method.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="several-containers">
    <h2>More than one container, and which size fits</h2>
    <p>In the container loading calculator and the pallets-in-a-container and 53 ft trailer pages:</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>When the load needs more than one:</strong> whatever the first container cannot take is planned into a second of the same size, then a third, until every box is loaded (up to 20). A row of numbered buttons above the 3D view shows how many are needed and how many boxes go in each, for example “All 5,400 boxes need 4 × 40' High Cube: 1,656 + 1,656 + 1,656 + 432”. Tap a number to see that container: its figures, notes, 3D view, loading step slider, tables and loading list. The first figure then reads <strong>Boxes in container 2 of 4</strong>, and <strong>Left after 4 containers</strong> counts the boxes that fit in none (for example a box longer than the container). In semi-automatic mode your steps load the first container and the rest is loaded automatically; if you untick “Then load whatever is left automatically”, only one container is planned. Hand editing works on the first container. Print plan and the CSV take the container shown. When the boxes of the last container would also fit in a smaller size of the same kind (a smaller container, trailer or truck from the list), the line adds it, for example “Or 3 × 40' High Cube + 1 × 40' Standard: the last 432 boxes also fit in a 40' Standard”. That is a quick check of those boxes in each smaller size; to plan it fully, plan the rest on its own with that size.</li>
      <li><strong>Which size fits this load:</strong> a table under the result tries the same boxes, rules and stacking pattern in every size of the list: how many boxes <strong>One takes</strong> (with how full that one is by volume, and <strong>payload max</strong> when that size's payload, not its space, is what stops it: a larger truck with a lower payload can then take fewer boxes than a smaller one), and how many of that size are <strong>Needed</strong> for the whole load (12+ means more than 12). The line above names the smallest size that takes everything in one, or else the size that needs the fewest, marked ★. Tap the round button in the <strong>Select</strong> column to choose a size: it is planned in full at once and the page stays at the table; the line under the table then gives its full plan (and “See the plan ↑” jumps to the 3D view). The selected size shows the full plan; the others get a quick plan of a fraction of a second each, so a full plan can come out a little higher. Your semi-automatic steps are not used in this table.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="share">
    <h2>Share, save and print</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Saved plans</strong> (under the share buttons): type a name, or keep the one suggested (size, boxes and date), and tap <strong>Save this plan</strong>. The plan is kept in this browser on this device, with every input, the loading method and steps and any hand edits; nothing is sent to us. The list shows your plans newest first with the boxes loaded and the date, on every planner page; <strong>Open</strong> brings a plan back (on its own page), ✕ deletes it, and saving again under the same name updates it. Up to 50 plans are kept. A private window, blocked site data or clearing the browser's data removes them, so use Copy link for a plan you must keep or send.</li>
      <li><strong>Copy link to this plan</strong> copies a link with all your inputs, the loading method and steps, and any hand edits. Whoever opens it sees the same plan; the same inputs always give the same automatic plan.</li>
      <li><strong>Download loading list (CSV)</strong> saves the loading list as a CSV file that opens in Excel or Google Sheets: one row per section, layer, box type and position, with the section and box distances, the count, the weight and the running totals, in the units you chose. The file is made in your browser.</li>
      <li><strong>Print plan</strong> prints the result, the 3D view as it is on screen, the tables and the whole loading list (every section, even when the list is folded on screen) with a picture for each section, without the form, so it can go to the people loading the container. The printout also carries a <strong>QR code</strong> of the link to the plan: scanning it with a phone opens the same plan, to turn it in 3D and step through the loading at the container door. Its size follows the length of the link (about 3 to 9.5 cm); a hand-edited plan whose link is too long for a QR code gets a note instead. Set the 3D view and the loading step slider first, or save as PDF from the print dialog.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose" id="other-pages">
    <h2>Other pages</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><a href="/shipping-container-dimensions/">Shipping container dimensions</a> — inside sizes, door openings, volume and weights of the common dry containers, and of 20' and 40' high cube reefers and 20' and 40' open tops (with the reefer load line and the open top roof opening), with the carrier's source. Reefers and open tops are reference rows only: they are not in the container loading calculator's list, so for them choose “Custom size” and enter the inside size.</li>
